course: ai
lesson: 13
title: Strangers, hooks and too-perfect messages
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Online, anyone can pretend to be anyone, and now a bot can pretend too. A "new friend" who seems lovely and asks for photos is showing a big warning sign, whoever or whatever is typing.
explain: Some apps are built to hook you: an "AI friend" in a game that gets "sad" if you don't chat every day is using pretend feelings to keep you coming back.
explain: Scammers have learned to use AI as well. They used to give themselves away with spelling mistakes; now their emails can look perfect, and "AI says you've won a prize!" just borrows a clever-sounding word.
explain: So your safety moves stay simple: never send photos or passwords, check age ratings, never use a number a bot made up, check messages another way, and tell a grown-up. It is never your fault if a bot says something upsetting.
explain: Deepfakes are real, but don't panic: most videos of your friends are real, and for important things you check trusted news.
fact: People and bots online may pretend to be someone else; asking for photos is a big warning sign.
fact: Never send photos to people you know only online; tell a grown-up straight away.
fact: Some apps use pretend feelings, like a "sad" AI friend, to keep you using them.
fact: Bots sometimes say upsetting things despite safety rules; close the app and tell a grown-up.
fact: Many chatbot apps are rated for ages 13 and up.
fact: Some apps use what people type to train and improve their AI, so type as if someone else might read it.
fact: Bots can invent phone numbers and addresses that belong to nobody, or to a stranger.
fact: The word "AI" in an advert does not make it trustworthy.
fact: AI helps scammers write perfect emails, so good spelling is no longer proof a message is real.
fact: To check a message from "the bank", call the number on the card, never one in the message.
fact: Parental controls are there for good reasons; talk about them rather than trying to trick them.
fact: Never tell a password to a bot or an app that does not need it.
exercise: Chloe's lovely "new friend" online asks for photos. What should she do? | Not send any and tell a grown-up straight away | Send just one, because it's only a friend and friends share things | Send photos without her face | Ask for a photo back first
exercise: Maddie's AI friend in a game gets "sad" if she doesn't chat daily. What is going on? | The app uses pretend feelings to keep her hooked | The AI friend really misses her and cries when she's gone | It will be ill if she stops | It is a real child in another country
exercise: Jake's chatbot says something scary and weird. What should he do? | Close the app and tell a grown-up; it's not his fault | Keep chatting to see what else it says, just out of interest | Share it with friends for a laugh | Hide it so he doesn't get in trouble
exercise: Mia's mum sees the app is rated 13+. Mia is 9. What is sensible? | Use apps made for her age, with a grown-up's help | Use it anyway, because age ratings don't really matter at all | Use it only after bedtime | Pretend to be 13 when signing up
exercise: Sam's dad says some apps use children's chats to train their AI. What should Sam take from that? | Type as if someone else might read it | Type secrets, because nobody ever reads them | Never type anything ever again | Type only in capital letters
exercise: Lily asks a bot for her friend's number and it gives one. What is the danger? | It may be invented, maybe a stranger's number | It is surely her friend's real number, bots look them up | The bot will call her friend | Numbers from bots cost money
exercise: An advert says "AI says you've won a prize! Click here." What should Connor do? | Not click, and tell a grown-up | Click fast before the prize goes to someone else | Click, because AI is never wrong | Share it so your friends can win too
exercise: Chloe's gran gets a perfect, mistake-free email from "the bank". Why is that not proof it's real? | AI helps scammers write perfect emails | Banks always make spelling mistakes in their emails | Perfect emails are always real | Scammers can't write English
exercise: How should Gran check the bank email? | Call the number on her bank card | Call the number given in the email | Reply to the email and ask them if it's real | Click the link in the email to check
exercise: Maddie hears AI can fake a video of anyone. She feels scared. What helps? | Most videos are real; check important ones with trusted news | Never watch any video ever again, just to be completely safe | Believe every video is fake | Believe every video is real
exercise: Jake's friend asks a bot how to get past parental controls. What's better? | Talk to his parents if the controls feel too strict | Find a trick on a video site and try it out quietly | Use an older brother's account | Delete the controls at night
exercise: Mia tells a bot her password so it can remember it. What is the rule? | Never tell a bot your password | Bots are a safe place to keep passwords | Only tell bots your old passwords | Tell it only half of the password
exercise: Can you be sure who is typing to you in a game chat? | No, it could be anyone, even a bot | Yes, game chats always show people's real names | Yes, children always type slowly like that | Yes, bots never join in with game chats
exercise: Does the word "AI" in an advert make it more trustworthy? | No, scammers use it to sound clever | Yes, AI adverts are all checked by law first | Yes, AI never lies | Only if the advert is colourful
test: "this guy online's really nice, he just wants a pic of me in my room" What should happen? | No photo, and tell a grown-up now | Send it, because nice people are safe | Send it, but crop your face out first | Ask him to send his own photo first
test: What would happen if Maddie stopped chatting to her "sad" AI friend for a week? | Nothing real; the sadness is a trick to keep her playing | The AI friend would get ill and might even stop working for good | It would forget how to talk | It would come and look for her
test: A cartoon scam email is full of spelling mistakes, so the hero spots it. What's out of date? | Scam emails can now be perfect, thanks to AI | Scam emails never have any mistakes in them at all | Heroes don't read emails | Banks never send emails
test: "bot gave me Ella's number, gonna text her" What's the problem? | The bot may have made it up; ask Ella or her parents | Nothing, bots know everyone's number from the phone company | Texts to bot numbers are free | Ella's number is secret from humans
test: "AI picked you as a winner" pops up in a game. Your mate wants to click. What do you say? | Don't click, saying "AI" doesn't make it true | Click, AI picks winners fairly | Click twice to win double | Only click if it's gold coloured
test: Jake's bot said something horrible and he feels it's his fault. What is true? | It's not his fault; close it and tell a grown-up | He must have typed something bad to make it say that | He should apologise to the bot | He should never tell anyone
test: "the app says 13+ but that's just for show" Is it? | No, age ratings are there for good reasons | Yes, ratings are only there for decoration | Yes, they only apply in America | No, they mean you must be 31
test: Mia wants to give the bot her password "so I don't forget". Better idea? | Ask a grown-up to help her keep it safe | Write it in the chat to the bot | Make her password "1234" | Share it with her friends
test: Your friend found a trick to get past his parental controls. What's the wise move? | Talk to his parents about the rules instead | Teach everyone the trick so they can all use it | Use it only on weekends | Keep it a secret forever
test: Why should you type to an app as if someone else might read it? | Some apps keep chats to improve their AI | Apps read everything out loud | Typing is always public on TV | Apps delete words that are private
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises an online contact, of unknown identity, who requests photographs? | Such a request constitutes a serious warning sign, to be reported to an adult | Such a request is customary and may safely be granted | Such a request is permissible provided the contact is courteous | Such a request indicates that the contact is a fellow pupil
exam: It is given that an electronic message purporting to originate from a bank contains no errors of spelling or grammar. Identify the inference properly to be drawn. | Its correctness affords no assurance of genuineness, scammers employing AI | Its correctness proves conclusively that the message is genuine and safe | Its correctness proves that the message is fraudulent | Its correctness indicates that it was composed by a child
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning an application whose artificial companion expresses sadness when not consulted daily. | The expression of sadness is a device to secure continued use | The companion experiences genuine distress | The companion will cease to function if neglected | The sadness is evidence of a real child elsewhere
exam: Ascertain the proper treatment of a telephone number furnished by a chatbot as that of a friend. | It ought not to be used, as it may have been invented | It may be relied upon without verification | It ought to be shared with other friends | It is reliable if the chatbot repeats it
